<Base title="Build with agents"><article class="prose-page"><h1>You set the direction.<br />The system helps it travel.</h1><p>Working with agents starts with making your intent clear. Show the references, explain what matters, and review what comes back. A good system gives that conversation somewhere to live.</p><h2>Give the work a starting point</h2><p>Ask your agent to read <a href="/llms.txt">the library index</a>, then the Markdown reference for the components it needs. Each reference carries the same props and guidance as the page you read here.</p><pre class="code-frame">Read the Diorama UI reference for the components this screen needs. Use the existing tokens and composition patterns. Explain any missing piece before introducing a new one.</pre><h2>Keep design, code and docs in conversation</h2><p>A working prototype will teach you things a drawing cannot. Bring those findings back to the design, refine them, and carry the decision into code and documentation. If a visual change gets lost in a long coding conversation, return to the canvas and make the intent visible.</p><h2>Turn feedback into something reusable</h2><p>At the end of a session, ask what should become part of the system. A useful correction might become a type, a check, a convention or a focused skill. That is how the next attempt starts closer to your intent.</p><p>Agents are useful for structured work: tokens, variants, implementation and verification. Your judgment still decides whether the result feels right and whether it solves the problem.</p><h2>Use the source</h2><p><a href="/llms-full.txt">The complete Markdown reference</a> is available as a single file. The <a href="/r/registry.json">registry index</a> lists installable items. Both are generated from the same repository as these pages.</p><p><a href={copy.article}>Read Jakub’s article about this workflow on Codrops ↗</a></p></article></Base>
